---
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const baseUrl = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : rawBase + '/';

const predicted = [
  {
    num: '3.1 %',
    label: 'predicted per-drop MAPE, pooled across 20 / 50 / 100 mL·h⁻¹.',
    tone: 'flow',
  },
  {
    num: '±3.87 µL',
    label: '95 % Limits of Agreement on 50 µL drops, committed to git before any bench data exists.',
    tone: 'flow',
  },
  {
    num: '49 %',
    label: 'of total predicted error attributable to print tolerance on the sensor arm — the dominant Rev-C lever.',
    tone: 'ir',
  },
  {
    num: '2.7 %',
    label: 'V_cal uncertainty at CAL_N = 10 (35 s of calibration time at 50 mL·h⁻¹) — drip-set-CV-limited.',
    tone: 'flow',
  },
];

const panels = [
  {
    label: '(a) Ablation',
    title: 'Which noise source carries the budget?',
    body: 'Each bar shows the pooled MAPE that would remain if that one noise source were forced to zero. Shorter bar = bigger contribution. Colour intensifies with attributable error (deepest red = the source to fix). Print tolerance dominates because the firmware uses a nominal d = 10.0 mm while individual printed sensor arms scatter ±0.115 mm 1-sigma; ADC discretisation and timing jitter are second-order; drop-volume CV and drop-velocity CV contribute essentially zero — a consequence of the dual-beam ratio inversion (see Findings).',
  },
  {
    label: '(b) Bland-Altman',
    title: 'Predicted device-vs-truth agreement.',
    body: 'Each dot is one of ~225 000 simulated drops. Y = (device estimate − true volume) in µL; X = mean of the two methods (the Bland-Altman convention). The black line is the predicted bias (+0.05 µL — effectively zero, no systematic skew); red dashes mark the 95 % Limits of Agreement (±3.87 µL). The three flow-rate colours overlap because the model predicts no rate-dependent failure mode. Post-bench drops will be overlaid as black triangles; landing them inside the red band validates the prediction.',
  },
  {
    label: '(c) Convergence',
    title: 'How many drops to lock V_cal?',
    body: 'The firmware boot sequence collects N drops, trims one each end, averages the rest. The curve shows SD(V_cal) as a function of N — for one board calibrating against its own drops. The firmware default CAL_N = 10 (red dashed) lands at 2.67 %; 35 s of calibration time at 50 mL·h⁻¹. The asymptote is set by the inherent ~8 % drop-to-drop variability of the drip set itself, not by sensor noise. The 1 % target line (grey dotted) is reachable but requires ~65 drops.',
  },
];

const findings = [
  {
    head: 'The dual-beam (τ / Δt) ratio inversion is velocity-invariant.',
    body: 'The firmware computes drop diameter as D = d_nominal × (τ / Δt). Both τ (shadow duration) and Δt (inter-beam transit) scale inversely with the drop\'s velocity, so the ratio cancels it exactly. The device does not need to know how fast a drop is falling to estimate its volume. The error budget surfaces this directly — drop-velocity CV contributes ≈ 0 percentage points to MAPE despite a realistic 8 % drop-velocity scatter in the inputs.',
  },
  {
    head: 'Print tolerance is the single knob worth turning for Rev-C accuracy.',
    body: 'Of the 3.13 % predicted MAPE, 1.54 percentage points (49 %) are attributable to per-board variation in the printed sensor arm. ADC discretisation contributes 0.28 pp; timing jitter 0.13 pp. Two design moves shrink the dominant term: storing per-board measured d_actual in flash (drops it to caliper-repeatability ≈ 0.05 mm SD), or injection-moulding the sensor arm (≈ ±0.05 mm tolerance). Either yields a ~4× reduction.',
  },
  {
    head: 'Calibration accuracy is drip-set-physics-limited, not sensor-limited.',
    body: 'The convergence asymptote is dictated by the ~8 % drop-volume coefficient of variation that any standard drip set exhibits — physical heterogeneity of real drops, not measurement error. Halving the calibration uncertainty requires ~4× more drops, or a more consistent drip set. The architecture is measuring better than its input variability allows.',
  },
  {
    head: 'Tethered power scope.',
    body: 'The validation dataset is collected with 3.3 V supplied directly to MCU VDD via ST-Link, bypassing the TPS610982 boost converter. The dataset validates the optical detection chain, sphere-model calibration, and dual-beam architecture. The field power chain (single AA Li-ion → boost → MCU) is deferred to Rev-C. This is named as a limitation rather than hidden in the bias term.',
  },
];
---

<section id="analysis">
  <div class="eyebrow">Analysis</div>
  <h2>Predicted accuracy from first principles, before any bench data.</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    A Monte Carlo error budget propagates measurement uncertainty through the
    firmware's volume-inversion math to predict per-drop MAPE, Bland-Altman
    Limits of Agreement, and calibration convergence — committed to git
    <em>before</em> the validation campaign begins. Post-bench drops are then
    overlaid onto the same axes. The git timestamp on the predicted figure is
    what turns the bench result into a verification rather than a post-hoc fit.
  </p>

  <div class="analysis-stats">
    {predicted.map((s) => (
      <div class={`stat tone-${s.tone}`}>
        <div class="num">{s.num}</div>
        <div class="label">{s.label}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <figure class="analysis-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/fig_error_budget_with_bench.png`}
         alt="A priori MC error budget with bench overlay — ablation, predicted Bland-Altman with bench drops, calibration convergence." />
    <figcaption>
      A priori Monte Carlo error budget at d = 10 mm, 50 µL drops, 0.77 m·s⁻¹
      fall velocity, Bambu Lab print tolerance ±0.115 mm 1-sigma — now with the
      2026-05-13 bench drops (N = 737, v_true_per_run = 73.83, 39.26, 59.32,
      51.95 µL) overlaid on panel (b) as red triangles. The runs land along the
      predicted band; the 1.27× scalar correction (V_CAL_K) closes the
      4-run-mean residual to within rounding. Per-run residuals span ±27 % —
      see the Limitations section for why this is fundamental, and §17 for the
      position-dependence finding that's invisible at this single mount height.
      Source notebook: <code>analysis/notebooks/error_budget.ipynb</code>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="panel-walks">
    {panels.map((p) => (
      <div class="panel-walk">
        <div class="panel-label">{p.label}</div>
        <h3>{p.title}</h3>
        <p>{p.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h3 class="findings-heading">Sanity-check figures from the bench dataset</h3>
  <p class="lead">
    Two views that aren't strictly part of the predicted-vs-measured argument
    but answer "did the device + the protocol behave?" in a glance.
  </p>

  <figure class="analysis-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/cumulative_mass_v50.png`}
         alt="Four panels, one per V_50 paired run, showing gravimetric cumulative mass versus scale-clock time. Each panel includes a linear fit overlaid in orange, with drop count, total mass delta, mean drop volume, and flow rate annotated in the panel title." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Gravimetric integrity, per V_50 run.</strong> Mass-vs-time
      from the Mettler MS scale stream, one panel per paired run. The
      orange linear fit gives the run-mean flow rate; deviations from
      linearity flag flow-regime breaks. V_50_01, _02, _05 are smooth
      and linear; V_50_03 shows a visible stutter at ~70–90 s into the
      run (a real flow micro-stall, not a device defect — drop counts
      and gravimetric mass both reflect it).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="analysis-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/velocity_histogram_per_position.png`}
         alt="Histograms of drop velocity at the TOP beam, one color per mount position, all overlaid on the same axis. Different positions show different median velocities ranging from 0.73 to 1.32 meters per second." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Time-of-flight reliability across positions.</strong>
      Per-drop transit-velocity v<sub>TOP</sub> = L/Δt − ½g·Δt, by mount
      position. Different mounts produce different free-fall distances
      before the drop reaches TOP, so the velocity itself is position-
      dependent — but the dual-beam transit-time measurement reliably
      captures whatever velocity each drop has. Medians span 0.73–1.32 m/s
      across the nine distinct mounts in the campaign.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="findings-heading">Findings of architectural significance</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    {findings.map((f) => (
      <li>
        <strong>{f.head}</strong>
        <span>{f.body}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <div class="reproducibility">
    <div class="repro-label">Reproducibility</div>
    <p>
      The figure regenerates byte-identical from a fresh clone — no input data
      required. Fixed RNG seed (<code>20260513</code>, the bench date) keeps
      the prediction stable across re-runs until a parameter changes. The
      assumption table for every input is the docstring of
      <code>scripts.error_budget.default_params</code>; every value traces to
      a measurement, a datasheet, or a flagged placeholder.
    </p>
    <pre><code>cd analysis
docker compose up regenerate-error-budget</code></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  #analysis h2 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 40px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    max-width: 22ch;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  #analysis h3 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 22px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  #analysis em { color: var(--ir); font-style: normal; }

  .analysis-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 40px 0 56px;
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat {
    padding: 20px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat .num {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 34px;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat .label {
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat.tone-ir .num { color: var(--ir); }
  .analysis-stats .stat.tone-flow .num { color: var(--flow); }

  .analysis-figure {
    margin: 0 0 56px;
    background: #ffffff;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 18px;
  }
  .analysis-figure img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .analysis-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .analysis-figure figcaption code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    background: var(--bg-3);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .panel-walks {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 56px;
  }
  .panel-walk {
    padding: 22px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .panel-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    color: var(--ir);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .panel-walk p {
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 8px 0 0;
  }

  h3.findings-heading {
    font-size: 28px;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .findings {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 56px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
  }
  .findings li {
    padding: 18px 20px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ir);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
  }
  .findings li strong {
    display: block;
    color: var(--fg);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .findings li span {
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .reproducibility {
    padding: 24px;
    background: var(--bg-3);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .repro-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    color: var(--flow);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .reproducibility p {
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .reproducibility p code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--fg);
    background: var(--bg-2);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .reproducibility pre {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 6px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .analysis-stats { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
    .panel-walks { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 540px) {
    .analysis-stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    #analysis h2 { font-size: 32px; }
  }
</style>
